.Dd August 17, 2026
.Dt INITTAB 5
.Os Sowa
.Sh NAME
.Nm inittab
.Nd what init starts, and when
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.Pa /etc/inittab
.Sh DESCRIPTION
.Pa /etc/inittab
is the whole of
.Xr init 8 Ns 's
configuration.
Each line is a colon-separated record:
.Pp
.Dl id : runlevels : action : process
.Pp
Blank lines and lines whose first non-blank character is
.Sq #
are ignored.
The fields are:
.Bl -tag -width runlevels
.It Ar id
Up to eight characters, unique in the file.
It is how
.Xr init 8
tells one entry from another across a reread, so keeping it stable across an
edit matters more than what it says.
.It Ar runlevels
The runlevels the entry belongs to, written together with no separator:
.Ql 12345 .
Empty means every runlevel.
.It Ar action
What
.Xr init 8
should do with the process; see below.
.It Ar process
The command.
It is executed directly unless it contains a shell metacharacter, in which case
.Pa /bin/sh -c
runs it.
A leading
.Sq @
is not part of the command: it tells
.Xr init 8
to give the process no terminal at all, which is what a getty needs, because a
getty opens its own device and makes it the controlling terminal of the session
it hands to
.Xr login 1 .
An entry already holding
.Pa /dev/console
could not do that.
.El
.Ss Actions
.Bl -tag -width powerokwait
.It Cm initdefault
The runlevel to enter at boot.
The process field is not used.
Without this entry
.Xr init 8
has nothing to enter and the boot goes nowhere.
.It Cm sysinit
Run once, before any runlevel, whatever the runlevel is.
.Xr init 8
waits for it.
.It Cm boot
Run once at boot, without waiting.
.It Cm bootwait
Run once at boot, and wait for it.
.It Cm wait
Run on entering one of the entry's runlevels, and wait for it.
This is what the
.Pa /etc/rc.d/rc
entries use.
.It Cm once
Run on entering one of the entry's runlevels, without waiting, and do not
restart it.
.It Cm respawn
Run on entering one of the entry's runlevels, and start it again whenever it
exits.
A respawn entry that starts more than ten times in two minutes is disabled for
five minutes, with a line on the console, and then tried again.
.It Cm ondemand
Run when
.Xr telinit 8
is asked for
.Cm a ,
.Cm b
or
.Cm c ,
which are not runlevels: the current runlevel does not change and nothing else
is started or stopped.
The entry's runlevel field holds those letters rather than digits.
.It Cm off
Ignore the entry.
.It Cm ctrlaltdel
Run when the kernel reports Ctrl-Alt-Del, which it does by sending
.Xr init 8
a
.Dv SIGINT .
.It Cm powerfail
Run on
.Dv SIGPWR ,
without waiting.
.It Cm powerwait
Run on
.Dv SIGPWR ,
and wait for it.
.It Cm powerokwait
Run on
.Dv SIGPWR
when power has come back, and wait for it.
.It Cm kbrequest
Run when the kernel asks for the KeyboardSignal keymap action, which reaches
.Xr init 8
as a
.Dv SIGWINCH .
.El
.Ss Runlevels
.Bl -tag -width "S  " -compact -offset indent
.It 0
halt or power off
.It 1
single user with the rc scripts run: services stopped, console login
.It 2
multi user
.It 3
multi user, and the Sowa default
.It 4
multi user, free for local use
.It 5
multi user, free for local use
.It 6
reboot
.It S
single user without the rc scripts, which is what
.Cm single
on the kernel command line gets
.El
.Sh FILES
.Bl -tag -width "/etc/rc.d/rc.sysinit" -compact
.It Pa /etc/inittab
this file.
.It Pa /etc/rc.d/rc.sysinit
the
.Cm sysinit
entry in the shipped file.
.It Pa /etc/rc.d/rc
the runlevel driver the
.Cm wait
entries run.
.El
.Sh EXAMPLES
The shape of the shipped file, which is a complete configuration:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
id:3:initdefault:
si::sysinit:/etc/rc.d/rc.sysinit
l3:3:wait:/etc/rc.d/rc 3
su:S:respawn:/bin/sh -l
1:12345:respawn:@/usr/sbin/agetty --noclear -l /usr/bin/login tty1 38400 linux
s0:12345:respawn:@/usr/sbin/serial-getty ttyS0 115200
ca::ctrlaltdel:/sbin/shutdown -r now
.Ed
.Pp
After editing the file, ask
.Xr init 8
to read it again.
Nothing is restarted by this; an entry whose command has changed is left alone
until it exits on its own:
.Pp
.Dl telinit q
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr chkconfig 8 ,
.Xr init 8 ,
.Xr serial-getty 8 ,
.Xr service 8 ,
.Xr telinit 8
.Sh CAVEATS
The file is
.Xr init 8 Ns 's
only configuration and it is read at boot before anything else exists, so a
line that cannot be parsed is a machine that may not reach a login prompt.
Booting with
.Cm emergency
on the kernel command line gets a root shell without reading this file at all,
which is how a bad edit is undone.
